\f1\fs22 The nefarious Halo and his rapy superpowers would have to wait for now. Finding Sarah took precedence.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Hayley rolled her eyes; not because she found no crime scene tape across the threshold of Tony\'92s apartment, but because the door was wide open. She doubted any investigators or forensics had visited the place. With any luck, the condition of the place could be exactly as Tony had left it, which made the open door an ominous sign. So too, the grimy yellow lighting, which was on despite the hour being well after sunrise. A dim narrow hall ended in the promise of opening onto an almost as dim living space. On the left an arch exposed a tired kitchen; a tall status closet and cooking stations on the left; cupboards under a bench opening into the living area. The back of two high chairs rammed against the bench from the other side, beyond those Hayley saw a cheap four-seater dining setting cramped against floor-to-ceiling curtains with dirty frayed hems and threads pulled everywhere. Grey under-Layer daylight oozed through from outside.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Hayley opened the stasis closet and winced. There was something rotten in there, which could happen only if it was left out to spoil first. Bananas. She peered through the door at the end of the kitchen: a shallow unlit space less than a pace deep and two wide; shelves of pantry staples; bottles of cleaning fluids; broom; mop and bucket, liquor bottles, crumpled boxes shoved high in the shelves, for forgetting the embarrassment of their contents.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>The air smelled like stale beer. Hayley found the reason why, in the living area of the living/dining room. A case of twenty-four tins lay crash-landed on the carpet, one corner completely crushed from impact, wet from leakage, a neat circular hole through one broad side. Hayley tilted the carton. On the side opposite the hole was a ragged gash ringed by soggy cardboard.\

\f2\i <$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Particle pistol
\f1\i0 , Hayley assessed. Either a government company agent or organised crime goon. She shook her head; they were the same, both so complacent and arrogant. Whoever it was, they sent no one back to clean up.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>The couch sat at a deranged angle,  back corner compressed against the window behind the curtains. A spatter of blood cried on the couch back. Hayley looked back at the shattered case of beer and surveyed the room: no other obvious sign of bloodshed anywhere.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>The state of the bedroom almost gave Hayley vertigo; so neat, she felt as she\'92d crossed space and time when she walked through the doorway. The living space was caveman chic crossed with saloon bar gunfight (the leaking beer didn\'92t help); the bedroom was almost Zen with its crisply made bed and designer-fluffed pillows. The bedroom was obviously the work of feminine-spectrum comfiness, the living room the work of masculine senselessness. In the wardrobes, Hayley found neatly folded, freshly pressed, though faded and threadbare, clothing, all men\'92s: tees, jeans, overalls with 
\fs18 CRACKER JACK CLEANING SERVICE
\fs20  embroidered on the chest pocket. In a drawer, underwear was casually thrown, loose socks and under wear tossed like salad greens. Hayley smiled at the five or six ratty pairs of pink boxer shorts with cute cartoon fairies on them.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Hayley\'92s eyebrows climbed almost to her hairline when she saw the small Airborne-brand overnight bag. It was resting squarely and purposefully in a cleared space on the floor of the built-ins. Work boots and running shoes were shoved aside and piled up so a fifteen-centimetre-wide swatch of carpet served as a protection zone for the bag. Hayley took the bag by the handles, tossed it lightly on the bed. It was almost empty\'97just one change of feminine blouse, pants and underwear, well perfumed\'97tossed as if already worn. Hayley ran her gloved fingertips around the inside lining of the bag, found a few stands of blonde hair which she evidence-bagged.
\fs22 \
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>The bathroom was clean and smelled of the same expensive perfume as the overnight bag, and was that vodka? The toilet was spotless, with no sign of male spray around it on the floor. The medicine cabinet was replete with creams and unguents (expensive Airborne brands for treating burns), clean bandages, and unopened packets of toothbrushes. A shiny set of toenail clippers rested on the sink. A bin by the cabinet was lightly stuffed with used bandages and swabs, bloody and gooey.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>It would seem, if Supaporn was the nightclub bomber, he managed to injure himself in the process. A bumbling cleaner fitted neither the profile of a terrorist nor a methodically precise serial killer. How could one person be a bomber 
\f2\i and
\f1\i0  the Nightclub Vampire? All of Hayley\'92s experience as an investigator discarded the improbability.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>But what of Sarah Jacobson\'92s disappearance? Was she a kidnap victim or another notch on the Nightclub Vampire\'92s triangular spike? Or neither?\

\f2\i <$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Sukrit Supaporn and Sarah Jacobson
\f1\i0 , Hayley thought. 
\f2\i Sarah Jacobson and Tony Supaporn. Was she his girlfriend
\f1\i0 ? Hayley thought it unlikely. Something else was afoot. There were at least two frequenters of this apartment\'97Hayley knew better than to assume a man and a woman. Could Supaporn incarcerate Jacobson here as kidnap victim? Then what of the presentability of the bedroom and bathroom? A prisoner would surely not do the housework\'97would they?\'97unless Jacobson needed something to occupy her idle hours of imprisonment. But if, Jacobson was a prisoner here, where did Tony sleep? Hayley found no evidence of spare pillows and blankets in the living room, so where did Supaporn sleep, if his bedroom had become Jacobson\'92s jail cell? More likely, Jacobson came here voluntarily, and came and went as she pleased, and was perhaps, given the Airborne medical supplies in the bathroom, nursing Tony after the bombing at the 
\f2\i Stickett Inn
\f1\i0 .\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>In the garage, Hayley found a vacant parking space with a secured cage in back that housed cleaning supplies. A cart with the same logo as on Supaporn\'92s overalls. A pair of arcs of relatively fresh tyre rubber ran along the concrete floor like railway tracks from the parking space, stopped suddenly then ran for a few metres towards the exit of the underground garage.
